      "name": "Research.Horner.Q",
      "description": "Q : Research.FloatingPoint.Model x List(R) x R -> R. For every model with rounding function r and every x in R, Q(model,[],x) = 0. For every a in R and finite real list t, Q(model,a::t,x) = r(a + r(x Q(model,t,x))). The tail is recursively computed with the same model and x, its product with x is rounded, a is added, and that sum is rounded. Every nonempty-list step has these two rounding applications, including the final coefficient step whose tail value is 0; the last coefficient is not simply returned. Neither x nor the coefficients are separately rounded. This is the actual computation compared with P by the theorem."
    },
    {
      "name": "Research.Horner.E",
      "description": "E : List(R) x R x R -> R is defined for all finite real lists and all real x and u. E([],x,u) = 0. For every a in R and finite real list t, let v = P(t,x) and e = E(t,x,u). Then E(a::t,x,u) = u |a + x v| + (1 + u)(u |x v| + (1 + u)|x| e). The local terms use the exact tail value P(t,x), not the computed Q. The definition allows arbitrary real u, including negative u; the target theorem uses it only at the nonnegative model parameter model.u, as an upper bound for |Q - P|."
    }
  ],
